---
import BrandGuide from '../../components/BrandGuide.astro';
import AffLink from '../../components/AffLink.astro';
import type { ConversionTable } from '../../config/sizeConversions';

const linkClass =
  'font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum';

const fitSections = [
  {
    category: "The bottoms charts don't agree with each other",
    text: [
      `Gap publishes two separate charts that translate women's bottoms sizes into men's: one on the women's bottoms guide and one on the men's, both behind <a href="https://www.gap.com/customer-service/size-charts?cid=2103" class="${linkClass}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gap's own size chart page</a> (captured August 2026), which every figure on this page is drawn from. They give different answers for the same body, and they do it consistently, one full size apart across the entire range.`,
      {
        ariaLabel: "Women's to men's bottoms sizes compared",
        minWidth: 520,
        headers: ["Your women's size", 'Waist', "Women's chart says", "Men's chart says"],
        rows: [
          ['8/29', '29½"', "Men's M", "Men's S"],
          ['12/31', '32"', "Men's L", "Men's M"],
          ['16/33', '35½"', "Men's XL", "Men's L"],
        ],
        noteAfter: true,
        note: `The tops charts do not have this problem. Gap's women's tops guide maps a women's L to a men's M, and the men's tops guide maps a men's M back to a women's L. They agree at every size. Whatever went wrong here is specific to bottoms.`,
      },
      `Which one to trust depends on what you are buying. If you are a woman buying a men's or unisex bottom, the men's chart is built around the men's waist measurement you will actually be selecting, so it is the safer reference. The practical version: <strong>match your waist measurement to the men's waist column and ignore the letter size on both charts.</strong>`,
    ],
  },
  {
    category: "Petite and tall change one measurement, and it isn't the one you think",
    text: [
      `Gap's women's tops chart has four tabs: regular, petite, tall, and unisex. Compare the regular and petite tables measurement by measurement and something becomes obvious. Bust and waist are identical. Not similar, identical, at every one of the fourteen sizes from 000 to 22. The same is true of the tall table. The only row that changes is sleeve length.`,
      {
        ariaLabel: 'Sleeve length by fit',
        minWidth: 440,
        headers: ['Size', 'Regular', 'Petite', 'Tall'],
        rows: [
          ['000', '30½', '29', '32½'],
          ['4', '31¼', '29¾', '33¼'],
          ['8', '31¾', '30¼', '33¾'],
          ['12', '32⅛', '30⅝', '34⅛'],
          ['16', '32¾', '31¼', '34¾'],
          ['20', '33½', '32', '35½'],
        ],
        noteAfter: true,
        note: `Sleeve length in inches. Petite runs 1½ inches shorter than regular, and tall runs 2 inches longer, at every size. The offsets are constant. Bust and waist rows are omitted deliberately: they are identical across all three tabs, which is the point.`,
      },
      `This matters because the standard advice about petite lines, including Gap's own marketing language, is that they are proportionally redesigned rather than simply shortened. Gap's published body measurements do not support that for tops. If you are choosing petite because your bust or waist sits between sizes, the petite chart will not help you. It is a <a href="/blog/petite-vs-regular-vs-tall/" class="${linkClass}">sleeve length decision</a>.`,
      `Bottoms work differently, and better. The women's bottoms guide offers five inseam lengths (petite 29", short 30", regular 31½", long 33½", tall 35½"), so length is a genuine independent variable there.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    category: 'Your jean number stops being your waist at size 12',
    text: [
      `Gap labels women's bottoms with a paired number, so a size 12 is written 12/31. The second number reads like a waist measurement in inches, and up to a point it behaves like one.`,
      `Through size 10, Gap's own chart puts your body waist about half an inch above the jean number. A 4/27 is a 27½ inch waist, an 8/29 is 29½. Consistent, and close enough that the label is useful.`,
      `Above size 10 the two come apart, and the gap widens with every size. This is <a href="/blog/vanity-sizing-explained/" class="${linkClass}">vanity sizing</a>, quantified from Gap's own published measurements.`,
      {
        ariaLabel: 'Gap size label versus measured waist',
        minWidth: 520,
        headers: ['Gap size', 'Jean number', 'Actual waist', 'Difference'],
        rows: [
          ['4/27', '27', '27½"', '+½"'],
          ['8/29', '29', '29½"', '+½"'],
          ['10/30', '30', '30½"', '+½"'],
          ['12/31', '31', '32"', '+1"'],
          ['14/32', '32', '33½"', '+1½"'],
          ['18/34', '34', '37½"', '+3½"'],
          ['22/36', '36', '41½"', '+5½"'],
        ],
        noteAfter: true,
        note: `By size 22/36 the label understates the body waist by five and a half inches.`,
      },
      `The practical consequence: <strong>if you wear size 12 or above, ignore the second number and match your measured waist to the chart.</strong> A shopper who reads 18/34 as "34 inch waist" and orders on that basis is off by three and a half inches, which is more than two full size grades at that end of the range.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    category: 'The accessories charts are a mess',
    text: [
      `Gap's women's hat chart lists S/M as 21½ inches wide and, on the same row, gives a Japan size of 52 to 54 centimetres. Twenty-one and a half inches is 54.6 centimetres, which is outside the range printed beside it. The L/XL row has the same problem: 22½ inches is 57.2 centimetres against a stated 54 to 56. Both rows are wrong in the same direction, so it is not a rounding artifact.`,
      `Gloves are measured differently by gender. The women's chart labels the measurement "Width" and runs 7 to 8. The men's chart labels it "Circumference Of Dominant Hand" and runs 8 to 10. Those are different quantities on overlapping scales, and one of the two labels has to be wrong.`,
      `The women's belt chart tops out at a 24 inch waist for XL. Gap's own women's apparel chart puts an XL waist at 35½ to 37½ inches. Whatever those belt figures describe, they do not correspond to any women's size in Gap's clothing range.`,
      `None of this stops you buying a hat. It is worth knowing because it tells you how much weight to put on the accessories charts generally, which is not much. Measure, then compare against the product page.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    category: "Men's charts, plainly",
    text: [
      `The men's charts are the most straightforward thing Gap publishes. Tops run from a 35 to 36 inch chest at XS to 49 to 52 at XXL, with neck and sleeve given as ranges alongside. Bottoms run from a 28 to 29 inch waist at XS to 42 to 45 at XXL. Both charts convert cleanly between inches and centimetres, and the men's tops chart agrees with the women's tops chart on cross-gender sizes.`,
      `The one asymmetry worth noting: men get two inseam lengths, regular at 32 inches and tall at 36. Women get five. If you are a man between those two lengths, Gap's own chart offers you nothing, and the answer is hemming.`,
    ],
  },
];

